# 15. Naranjo Algorithm Assessment

The Naranjo Adverse Drug Reaction Probability Scale can also be used as a structured method for assessing the probability that an adverse event is associated with a suspected medicine.

For this simulated case, the following assessment is made:

| Naranjo Question                                                             | Assessment                    | Score |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----: |
| Previous conclusive reports of this reaction?                                | Yes                           |    +1 |
| Did the event occur after the suspected drug was administered?               | Yes                           |    +2 |
| Did the reaction improve after the drug was discontinued?                    | Yes                           |    +1 |
| Did the reaction reappear after re-administration?                           | Not performed                 |     0 |
| Are there alternative causes?                                                | No apparent alternative cause |    +2 |
| Did the reaction appear with placebo?                                        | Not applicable                |     0 |
| Was drug detected in toxic concentrations?                                   | Not applicable                |     0 |
| Was the reaction dose-dependent?                                             | Not established               |     0 |
| Did the patient have a similar reaction to the same/similar drug previously? | No                            |     0 |
| Was the adverse event confirmed by objective evidence?                       | Clinical findings documented  |    +1 |

### Total Score

**Naranjo Score = 7**

### Interpretation

A score in this range is consistent with a **probable adverse drug reaction** under the Naranjo probability framework.

Therefore, both structured assessments support a probable relationship:

> **WHO-UMC: Probable/Likely**
> **Naranjo: Probable**

