# 21. Case Narrative

## Medical Narrative

A 32-year-old female patient weighing 61 kg was prescribed amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg orally three times daily for an acute bacterial respiratory tract infection.

The patient had no documented history of drug allergy and was not receiving any regular medication. Paracetamol 500 mg was used as required for fever/body ache.

The patient tolerated the initial doses of amoxicillin-clavulanate without a reported reaction. Approximately two hours after administration of the third dose on Day 2 of treatment, she developed generalized itching and widespread urticarial lesions.

Approximately 30 minutes later, she developed swelling of the lips and face and reported a sensation of throat tightness.

The suspected medicinThe suspected medicine was immediately discontinued and the patient was taken to an emergency department for evaluation. She received antihistamine and corticosteroid treatment with supportive care. Her symptoms improved substantially during observation and she was subsequently discharged in stable condition.

The suspected medicine was not reintroduced. The patient was reported to have recovered from the reaction during follow-up.

Based on the available information, the event was assessed as a serious and severe suspected hypersensitivity reaction. The temporal relationship, absence of a strong alternative explanation and improvement following withdrawal supported a probable/likely causal relationship according to the WHO-UMC framework.

