# 23. Conclusion 

This simulated case demonstrates the complete pharmacovigilance workflow for an Individual Case Safety Report.

The patient developed generalized urticaria, pruritus, lip/facial angioedema and throat tightness following administration of amoxicillin-clavulanate. The suspected medicine was discontinued and the patient improved following appropriate medical treatment.

The case was classified as serious because of the clinically significant reaction requiring urgent medical assessment and intervention. The reaction was classified as severe based on the intensity of the clinical manifestations.

The temporal relationship, positive dechallenge, absence of a strong alternative explanation and clinical plausibility support a causal relationship between amoxicillin-clavulanate and the reported reaction.

The final causality assessment was:

WHO-UMC: Probable/Likely

The Naranjo assessment also supported a:

Probable adverse drug reaction

The case demonstrates the responsibilities of a Drug Safety Associate, including case intake, case validity assessment, adverse event identification, seriousness and severity assessment, causality assessment, coding considerations, case narrative preparation, follow-up and appropriate ADR reporting.

