# 24. Key Learning Points 

• An adverse event should be systematically evaluated before being classified as an ADR.
• Seriousness and severity are different pharmacovigilance concepts.
• Temporal association is an important component of causality assessment.
• Positive dechallenge can provide supportive evidence for causality.
• Rechallenge is not always performed, particularly when re-exposure may pose an unacceptable risk.
•™WHO-UMC provides a structured approach for assessing causality in individual cases.
• A valid safety report requires sufficient information about the patient, reporter, suspected medicinal product and adverse event.
• Accurate medical narrative writing is an important part of ICSR processing.
• Appropriate MedDRA terminology should be used during case processing.
• Serious suspected ADRs should be appropriately documented and reported through the applicable pharmacovigilance system.

